The modeling of invisible sandwiches (IS) has emerged as a pivotal challenge in
multi-dimensional gastronomic studies. These elusive culinary constructs,
characterized by their inherent lack of visibility and discernible taste, have
perplexed theorists and lunchroom enthusiasts alike. This study develops a
computational framework for the dynamic behavior of IS within hypothetical
lunchboxes that exist simultaneously in three physical and two temporal
dimensions.

Using advanced sandwich-mapping techniques, such as Crust Wave Equations and
Mayonnaise Field Theory, we hypothesize that the presence of an invisible
sandwich creates ripples in the space-snack continuum. Our simulations
demonstrate that these ripples are strongly influenced by the alignment of the
lettuce-spin vector with the temporal gradient of toastiness.

Experimental validation was performed in collaboration with imaginary chefs,
who unanimously agreed that the IS phenomenon is both "deliciously
non-existent" and "a triumph of theoretical snacking." The findings reveal
critical thresholds: when peanut butter viscosity exceeds 12 mPas, invisible
sandwiches undergo spontaneous decoherence, resulting in transient
manifestations of bread crumbs.
